parameters:
* image_size: tuple (height, width) -- height first (exporters/__init__.py:102; save_export_manager.py:397, save_worker.py:86). h is the divisor for cy and nh, w for cx and nw (yolo_detection.py:20, 29-32). * Output path: os.path.splitext(image_path)[0] + '.txt' (yolo_detection.py:45-46) -- only the LAST extension is stripped, so a/b.tar.png -> a/b.tar.txt and a/b (no extension) -> a/b.txt. * Guards: return None (write nothing) if h <= 0 or w <= 0 (yolo_detection.py:21-22) -- '<= 0', not '== 0' -- and if no contour produced any annotation (yolo_detection.py:35-36). * File mode: open(path, 'w') -- truncate and overwrite; parent dir created via os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path) or '.', exist_ok=True) (yolo_detection.py:39-40). * Line: f'{class_id} {cx} {cy} {nw} {nh}' + '\n' -- exactly five fields, one U+0020 between each, no leading or trailing spaces, no header, no comments, no blank lines (yolo_detection.py:33, 41-42). Text mode maps '\n' to os.linesep, so CRLF on Windows and LF elsewhere. No encoding is passed; content is pure ASCII, so the locale default is irrelevant. * class_id: class_order[channel] as a decimal integer. class_order = sorted unique class ids present IN THIS IMAGE (segment_manager.py:87-95); channel = its index in that list (segment_manager.py:221, 276). * Box: cv2.boundingRect of the contour -- x,y = min column/row, bw,bh = inclusive pixel span (yolo_detection.py:27). A single pixel at (0,0) gives (0,0,1,1). * Math, in exactly this operation order (IEEE-754 doubles then match bit for bit): cx = (x + bw/2)/w; cy = (y + bh/2)/h; nw = bw/w; nh = bh/h (yolo_detection.py:29-32). Do NOT algebraically regroup to (2*x + bw)/(2*w). No rounding, no clamping, no epsilon. * Value ranges: cx and cy are strictly in (0,1); nw and nh are in (0,1] and are exactly 1.0 when the box spans the full width/height. * NUMBER FORMATTING (the part a TS port gets wrong by default). The legacy writer interpolates a Python float with str()/repr(): the shortest decimal digit string that round-trips to the same double, laid out by this rule -- let D be that digit string and decpt the decimal-point position (value = 0.D x 10^decpt). If decpt <= -4 or decpt > 16, Python emits exponential form d[.ddd]e followed by an ALWAYS-PRESENT sign and at least two zero-padded exponent digits ('1e-05', '6.103515625e-05', '1e+16'); otherwise positional form, with '.0' appended when there is no fractional part ('1.0', '0.0001'). JavaScript's String(v) uses the same digit string D but different layout rules: no '.0' on whole numbers, and exponential only below 1e-6 (and then with an unpadded exponent, '1e-7' vs Python '1e-07'). Measured over 8013 values produced by real (x+bw/2)/w and bw/w computations across image widths 1..1e7, String(v) differed from Python on 421 of them (416 whole numbers such as nw=1.0, 5 scientific such as 1e-05 and 9.999e-05). Never use toFixed, toPrecision or String(v). Use this function -- it reproduced Python's repr on all 8013 sampled values with zero mismatches:
function pyRepr(v: number): string {
  if (!Number.isFinite(v)) return Number.isNaN(v) ? 'nan' : (v > 0 ? 'inf' : '-inf');
  const neg = v < 0 || Object.is(v, -0);
  const a = Math.abs(v);
  if (a === 0) return neg ? '-0.0' : '0.0';
  const [mant, expStr] = a.toExponential().split('e'); // no arg => shortest round-trip digits
  const digits = mant.replace('.', '');
  const decpt = parseInt(expStr, 10) + 1;             // Python's decpt
  let out: string;
  if (decpt <= -4 || decpt > 16) {
    const m = digits.length > 1 ? digits[0] + '.' + digits.slice(1) : digits[0];
    const e = decpt - 1;
    out = m + 'e' + (e < 0 ? '-' : '+') + String(Math.abs(e)).padStart(2, '0');
  } else if (decpt <= 0) {
    out = '0.' + '0'.repeat(-decpt) + digits;
  } else if (decpt >= digits.length) {
    out = digits + '0'.repeat(decpt - digits.length) + '.0';
  } else {
    out = digits.slice(0, decpt) + '.' + digits.slice(decpt);
  }
  return neg ? '-' + out : out;
}
Realistic triggers: nw/nh = '1.0' whenever a mask touches both edges of the image (common: a loaded NPZ whole-class mask); scientific notation whenever a coordinate drops below 1e-4, i.e. cx for a 1px box at x=0 needs w > 5000 and nw for a 1px box needs w > 10000 (an 8192x8192 tile writes '0 6.103515625e-05 6.103515625e-05 0.0001220703125 0.0001220703125').
